---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import FeatureVisual from '../../components/FeatureVisual.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/siteUrl';
import '../../styles/docs-prose.css';
---

<Layout title="Settings & Preferences — Varve Docs" description="Varve settings: theme, units, snapping, performance options, shortcuts, and per-platform preferences.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / Settings & Preferences
      </nav>

      <h1 class="site-page-title">Settings & Preferences</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Varve's settings panel customizes editor behavior, appearance, export defaults, and the interval for automatic local backups. Automatic recovery is separate from saving a document to its chosen file or library destination.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Opening Settings</h2>
        <p>
          Access settings from the menubar: <strong>File &gt; Settings</strong> (or press the keyboard shortcut if configured). The settings dialog has several focused sections:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Appearance</strong> — Theme, workspace layout, grid, and snap preferences</li>
          <li><strong>Drawing Input</strong> — Finger ownership, wheel policy, sensitivity, and optional mouse-wheel continuation</li>
          <li><strong>Performance</strong> — Interactive preview quality, diagnostics, and platform capability checks</li>
          <li><strong>Export</strong> — Default export format, scale, ICC profile, bleed, and text outlining</li>
          <li><strong>Updates</strong> — Update consent, channel, and install-on-quit preferences on supported desktop builds</li>
          <li><strong>Plugins</strong> — Install local packages, review access, search descriptions and commands, pin frequently used plugins, manage failed or disabled packages, and recover updates. The manager stays available in safe mode. See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/plugins')}>plugin guide</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The section list has a filter: type part of a section name to narrow the list, and the
          dialog follows the first match so the relevant panel is already open when you stop typing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Updates</h2>
        <p>
          The Updates section controls whether Varve checks for newer desktop releases. Choose
          <strong>Manual</strong> to check only when you ask, <strong>Automatically check and
          notify me</strong> to receive availability notices, or automatic downloads when you
          explicitly want verified update files downloaded in the background. Installation and
          restart remain separate actions.
        </p>
        <p>
          These controls do not override package ownership. Debian/RPM installs, Microsoft Store
          installs, development builds, unsupported architectures, and read-only or translocated
          app copies remain outside the in-app updater. See the{' '}
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/updates')}>Updates guide</a> for the package matrix and privacy
          details.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Appearance Settings</h2>

        <h3>Theme</h3>
        <p>
          Varve ships with Light and Dark themes, each audited for WCAG AA contrast compliance. The
          website and editor present these as peer choices rather than an on/off switch. The
          desktop editor also includes High Contrast for accessibility.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Light</strong> — Clean white-based interface with teal accent</li>
          <li><strong>Dark</strong> — Dark background with lighter-on-dark hierarchy (front-lit: higher surfaces are brighter)</li>
          <li><strong>High Contrast</strong> — Maximum contrast mode for accessibility, with distinctive colors per layer type (e.g., purple for image layers)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          All themes use OKLCH color space for perceptually uniform color ramps. The 12-step neutral and teal ramps ensure consistent contrast across every surface and text combination.
        </p>

        <h3>Canvas Grid</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Pixel Grid</strong> — Toggle the pixel-level grid overlay on the canvas</li>
          <li><strong>Layout Grid</strong> — Toggle layout grid overlay for alignment reference</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Pointer snapping (Inspector → Document → Snapping)</h3>
        <p>
          Pointer-snap preferences live with the editor viewport, not in the document file. Open
          the Inspector's <strong>Document</strong> panel and expand <strong>Snapping</strong> to
          change them without changing the artwork.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Enable snapping</strong> — Turn magnetic pointer assistance on or off for eligible canvas tools</li>
          <li><strong>Tolerance</strong> — Set magnetic acquisition distance from 1–32 CSS pixels; zoom does not change the size of the hand movement</li>
          <li><strong>Object geometry</strong> — Include visible object edges and centers in the active editing scope</li>
          <li><strong>Pages and frames</strong> — Include page and containing-frame bounds</li>
          <li><strong>Guides</strong> — Include ruler and authored layout guides</li>
          <li><strong>Reset snapping</strong> — Restore these viewport preferences without changing document geometry</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Document-grid snap and integer pixel snap remain explicit controls in the separate
          <strong>Document Grid</strong> section. They are not substitutes for magnetic pointer
          snapping, and Arrow-key nudges never use the magnetic tolerance.
        </p>

        <h3>Nudging &amp; Movement</h3>
        <p>
          Set independent <strong>Small nudge</strong> and <strong>Big nudge</strong> amounts. Arrow
          uses the small amount and Shift+Arrow uses the big amount. Values may be positive
          fractions, are stored in canonical geometry units, and are displayed using the General
          unit preference. Reset restores the factory defaults without changing document geometry.
          Keyboard nudges are exact and do not invoke magnetic snapping or reparenting.
        </p>

        <h3>Alignment &amp; distribution</h3>
        <p>
          The Align &amp; Distribute bar keeps the reference and mode explicit. Choose selection
          bounds, a containing frame, a page, or a key object; then choose equal gaps, equal
          centers, fixed gap, or Tidy Up. Fixed gap is available for two eligible objects, while
          automatic equal-gap and equal-center distribution require three. See the
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/precision-placement')}>Precision Placement guide</a> for
          the exact geometry contracts.
        </p>

        <h3>Canvas Mode</h3>
        <p>
          The default canvas mode on startup: Full (all fills and effects), Outline (structure-only), or Preview (clean display).
        </p>

        <h3>Workspace layout</h3>
        <p>
          Choose <strong>Automatic</strong>, <strong>Tablet</strong>, or <strong>Desktop</strong>
          in Appearance settings, or from <strong>View &gt; Workspace layout</strong>. Automatic
          responds to the available CSS viewport width and pointer capabilities the browser
          reports; Varve does not detect a detachable keyboard or ChromeOS tablet-mode switch.
          This preference controls panel presentation and touch-target sizing independently of
          Drawing Input and density. It takes effect after active contacts and text composition
          finish so changing it does not interrupt an edit.
        </p>
        <h3>Mirror tablet controls</h3>
        <p>
          The Appearance switch places the Layers, Inspector, and Resources launchers on the
          opposite side of the canvas for left-handed or alternate reach. It is saved separately
          from the layout choice and does not change desktop panel docking.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" data-testid="navigation-settings-help">
        <h2>Navigation and rendering settings</h2>
        <h3>Drawing Input</h3>
        <p>
          The default wheel policy uses an unmodified wheel for canvas pan and
          Ctrl/Cmd+wheel for pointer-centred zoom. Choose <strong>Always pan</strong> or
          <strong>Always zoom</strong> when your mouse, trackpad, or WebView reports a gesture
          differently. The sensitivity control changes the normalized CSS-pixel travel and
          accepts fractional values from 0.25&times; to 4&times;.
        </p>
        <p>
          Optional detented mouse-wheel continuation is deliberately separate from trackpad
          momentum. Trackpad and ambiguous pixel streams remain direct-only so the editor does
          not add a second momentum tail. These preferences affect the view only: they never
          change artwork, selection, dirty state, or undo history.
        </p>

        <h3>Performance</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Automatic (recommended)</strong> lets the renderer choose a bounded movement
          quality from observed frame pressure. After input stops it requests an authoritative
          frame; the warm refinement targets are 500&nbsp;ms for ordinary work and 1&nbsp;s for
          heavy work after required assets are ready. These are targets, not guarantees; cold
          image or font readiness can extend the wait and is not quantified in the published
          evidence.
          <strong>Full resolution while navigating</strong> keeps the full device-pixel target
          during input and may reduce responsiveness on constrained hardware. A worker preview
          may briefly be reprojected while a replacement is pending. Neither choice changes
          saved artwork or the independent quality of export and print output.
        </p>
        <p>
          The diagnostics and platform capability report are opt-in and local. They show the
          active backend, frame age/cadence, queue state, preview tier, and fallback reason
          without collecting artwork or persistent hardware identifiers.
        </p>
        <FeatureVisual
          scene="performance-settings"
          sizes="(min-width: 1100px) 760px, 100vw"
          caption="The Performance dialog shows the current preview-quality control and opt-in local diagnostics. This is a capture from the running editor interface, not a mockup."
        />
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>How binary settings behave</h2>
        <p>
          A compact switch is reserved for a setting that is genuinely on or off, such as Show
          guides or Enable snapping. Canvas renderer is a peer choice: Canvas2D is the default,
          WebGPU is used when a usable device is available, and experimental WebGL2 currently
          handles eligible simple shapes and uncropped stretch image fills with Canvas2D replay
          for the rest. The active path can still be Canvas2D when a backend is unavailable or the
          artwork does not qualify.
          Supporting copy appears beside controls that have a consequence or dependency.
        </p>
        <p>
          Peer choices such as theme, color space, and canvas mode use a radio group, segmented
          control, or select. Checkboxes remain for multi-select lists and mixed values. Settings
          keep their existing persistence scope, and staged dialog changes still wait for Apply or
          Save.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Export Settings</h2>

        <h3>Default Format</h3>
        <p>
          Choose the default export format: PNG, SVG, or PDF. This preselected option appears first in the Export dialog.
        </p>

        <h3>Scale</h3>
        <p>
          Default raster export scale: 1x, 2x, or 3x. Higher scales produce larger images suitable for Retina/HiDPI displays.
        </p>

        <h3>PDF Export Defaults</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>ICC Profile</strong> — Default color profile for PDF output (sRGB, Adobe RGB, Fogra39, GRACoL, SWOP)</li>
          <li><strong>Bleed</strong> — Default bleed width in mm for print documents</li>
          <li><strong>Outline Text</strong> — Whether to convert text to vector paths by default in PDF output</li>
          <li><strong>Rendering Intent</strong> — Perceptual, Relative Colorimetric, Absolute Colorimetric, or Saturation</li>
          <li><strong>Color Profile</strong> — Document color space for PDF (RGB or CMYK)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Naming</h3>
        <p>
          Configure the output file naming template. Use variables like <code>&lcub;name&rcub;</code>, <code>&lcub;format&rcub;</code>, <code>&lcub;scale&rcub;</code> to create consistent naming patterns.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Automatic backups and recovery</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Autosave interval (min)</strong> controls how often Varve writes an internal local copy while you edit. The setting accepts 1–60 minutes and defaults to 5 minutes. For a document with an app-library record, autosave updates that internal record and creates a recovery point; an untitled document is stored as a recovery point.
        </p>
        <p>
          An autosave is <strong>not</strong> a write to the file you chose, and it does not mark that file as saved. Use <strong>File &gt; Save</strong> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>/<kbd>Cmd</kbd>+<kbd>S</kbd>) to write to the current destination; the first save asks you to choose one. Check the status bar for <strong>Saved</strong>, <strong>Modified</strong>, or a save error before closing important work. Recovery points are a fallback, not an archival backup. See <a href={sitePath('/docs/file-formats')}>File Formats</a> for the save destinations and compatibility rules.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Loading and Task Feedback</h2>
        <p>
          Varve keeps feedback proportional to the work. Short actions use a small
          indeterminate indicator with a concise task label, while structured lists and
          grids preserve their shape with skeleton placeholders. Downloads, imports,
          exports, and other measurable operations show real progress and offer cancellation
          where supported.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Save and auto-save</strong> — Stay quiet during normal background work; a failure is surfaced with a clear recovery path.</li>
          <li><strong>Model and asset downloads</strong> — Show actual progress rather than an endless spinner.</li>
          <li><strong>Panel refreshes</strong> — Keep the existing content in place and use a contextual loading state instead of blocking the whole editor.</li>
          <li><strong>Startup</strong> — The branded startup loader is separate from in-editor task feedback and exits when the home surface is ready.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          These choices are especially important for Varve's local-first workflow: offline
          editing should feel immediate, fast actions should not flash a loader, and optional
          network or on-device model work should make its state and progress explicit.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Resetting Settings</h2>
        <p>
          If you need to start fresh, the settings dialog includes a "Reset to Defaults" button. This restores all settings to their factory defaults without affecting your documents.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Related Guides</h2>
        <p>See <a href={sitePath('/docs/file-formats')}>File Formats</a> for export format details, or <a href={sitePath('/docs/getting-started/interface')}>Interface Overview</a> for editor layout.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* The performance visual uses FeatureVisual's own framing; nothing
     page-specific is layered on top of it. */
</style>

<style>
  .docs-page {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 3rem 0;
  }
  .docs-page h1 { font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 144; letter-spacing: -0.015em; }

  .docs-section h2 { border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border-accent); padding-bottom: var(--space-3); }

</style>
